The woomera comes in aspect ratios from very long
and narrow, "club shaped", to moderately broad,
"narrow shield". The woomera also turns out to be
technically different from an atlatl. The woomera
has a tang that (apparently) fits into a socket in
the butt of the spear. The atlatl, in contrast, has
a socket or notch into which the butt of the spear
fits. Otherwise they seem to work the same way.

An assortment of "stuff" including several woomera,
both "fat" and "skinny".

http://www.primitivetribalart.com/Categories.aspx?id=9

An example slender one, clearly showing the peg or
tang used to mate the spear butt to the woomera
while the spear is being thrown.

http://www.primitivetribalart.com/Item.aspx?itemid=72

Atlatl are not "just" Central American tools; their use was
evident in North Carolina in the middle Archaic period
(6000-3000 bce) as well.

http://www.bragg.army.mil/culturalresources/prehistory.htm

Other links put them in Minnesota.

In the way of a "god of the atlatl" after whom to name an
artifact weapon, this seems like a good choice; meet Opochtli,
the "patron saint" of atlatls:

http://www.d.umn.edu/cla/faculty/troufs/anth1602/pcatlatl.html

